The quadrilateral ABCD is made up of two isosceles right triangles, ABC and ACD. The hypotenuse of triangle ABC is $\overline{BC}$, which has a length of 2 units. In an isosceles right triangle, the legs are equal in length and the hypotenuse is $\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore, the length of each leg, $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{AC}$, is $\frac{2}{\sqrt{2}} = \sqrt{2}$ units. The perimeter of quadrilateral ABCD is the sum of the lengths of all its sides: $\overline{AB}$, $\overline{BC}$, $\overline{CD}$, and $\overline{DA}$. We already know $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{AC}$, and since ABCD is made up of isosceles right triangles, $\overline{CD}$ and $\overline{DA}$ will also be $\sqrt{2}$ units each. Thus, the perimeter is $\sqrt{2} + 2 + \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{2} = 2 + 3\sqrt{2}$ units.</think></br></br> </br></br></br></br></br></br></br></br></br></thinking style='font-family: normal; duration: 10';</br></br></br></br></br></></break></br></break></br></break></break></br></break></br></break></break></br></ Broader, Generalized, and Theory Focused
